 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Evaluate anything inside Brackets first (innermost first)",
   "Evaluate Indices (powers and roots)",
   "Work out Division and Multiplication from left to right",
   "Work out Addition and Subtraction from left to right"
  ],
  "title": "Four Operations & Order of Operations",
  "content": "<p>The four operations are <strong>addition</strong>, <strong>subtraction</strong>, <strong>multiplication</strong> and <strong>division</strong>. When a calculation involves more than one operation, you must follow the correct <strong>order of operations</strong> — often remembered by the acronym <strong>BIDMAS</strong> (or BODMAS).</p><p><strong>B</strong>rackets first, then <strong>I</strong>ndices (powers and roots), then <strong>D</strong>ivision and <strong>M</strong>ultiplication (left to right), then <strong>A</strong>ddition and <strong>S</strong>ubtraction (left to right).</p><p>Division and multiplication have <em>equal</em> priority — work left to right. The same applies to addition and subtraction. Nested brackets are worked from the innermost pair outwards.</p><p>A common mistake is to add before you multiply, e.g. \\(2 + 3 \\times 4 = 14\\), NOT \\(20\\). Always scan the expression for higher-priority operations before evaluating.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Calculate</strong> \\(3 + 4 \\times 2^2 - (6 \\div 3)\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1 — Brackets:</strong> \\(6 \\div 3 = 2\\), so expression becomes \\(3 + 4 \\times 2^2 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2 — Indices:</strong> \\(2^2 = 4\\), so \\(3 + 4 \\times 4 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3 — Multiplication:</strong> \\(4 \\times 4 = 16\\), so \\(3 + 16 - 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 4 — Addition/Subtraction (left to right):</strong> \\(3 + 16 = 19\\), then \\(19 - 2 = 17\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(17\\)</p>"
 },
